            <ArticleTitle>[Connection of geogeophysical factors and the course of unstable angina pectoris].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>34-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To study relationships between changes in solar and geomagnetic activity and a course of unstable angina pectoris with neuron nets.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Heliogeophysical variables were studied in 67 male patients at the age of 42 to 75 years with unstable angina pectoris.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The neuronetwork analysis has revealed marked non-linear relations between angina episodes and heliogeophysical factors.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Small-amplitude changes in solar and geomagnetic activity may influence the course of unstable angina pectoris.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
